> planner/workflow/delivery.py
> """
> Async result delivery policy and user-visible update creation.
> 
> Every completed operation may update durable state. Only explicitly eligible
> results may create visible assistant messages. Do not generate noisy progress
> messages or silently revise earlier answers.
> 
> Public API:
> 
> class DeliveryService:
>     def consider_delivery(
>         self,
>         workflow: Workflow,
>         artifacts: Sequence[Artifact],
>         *,
>         original_response_id: str | None,
>     ) -> AsyncUpdate | None:
>         '''
>         Apply DeliveryPolicy: never, on_material_result, on_completion,
>         on_user_request, or digest. Enforce opt-in, quiet hours, materiality,
>         and max-update limits.
>         '''
>         ...
> 
>     def deliver(self, update: AsyncUpdate) -> None: ...
> 
> Visible updates must:
> - be ordinary new assistant messages;
> - identify their relation to the originating turn/response;
> - identify correction or supersession where applicable;
> - preserve links to operations and artifacts;
> - explain why delivery occurred.
> 
> Caller:
> - workers/scheduler call `consider_delivery` after results are persisted.
> - async_api.py exposes delivered-but-unread updates through `poll()`.
> """
> 
> =====
> planner/workflow/models.py
> """
> Workflow-DAG domain types.
> 
> A Plan is intended policy. An Operation is an executable unit. An Artifact is
> durable output. Dependencies define readiness. Continuations run after needed
> artifacts resolve. Subscriptions/watch rules react to future events.
> 
> Expected types:
> 
> @dataclass
> class Operation:
>     id: str
>     workflow_id: str
>     kind: str                 # model_call, tool_call, evaluator, job
>     status: Literal["pending", "ready", "running", "completed", "failed", "cancelled"]
>     plan_id: str | None
>     input: Mapping[str, Any]
>     dependency_ids: list[str]
>     artifact_ids: list[str]
>     created_at: float
>     started_at: float | None = None
>     completed_at: float | None = None
>     error: str | None = None
>     retry_count: int = 0
> 
> @dataclass
> class Artifact:
>     id: str
>     conversation_id: str
>     operation_id: str
>     kind: str                 # source_bundle, retrieval, draft, code_result, image, ...
>     content: Any
>     provenance: Provenance
>     created_at: float
>     expires_at: float | None = None
>     durable: bool = True
> 
> @dataclass
> class SourceExcerpt:
>     id: str
>     artifact_id: str
>     text: str
>     source_locator: str | None
>     relevance_score: float | None
>     provenance: Provenance
> 
> @dataclass
> class Workflow:
>     id: str
>     conversation_id: str
>     originating_turn_id: str | None
>     plan: PlanSpec
>     operations: dict[str, Operation]
>     delivery_policy: DeliveryPolicy
> 
> @dataclass
> class StatePatch:
>     scope: Literal["turn", "conversation", "project", "user_memory"]
>     target_id: str
>     patch: list[JsonPatchOp]
>     provenance: Provenance
>     confidence: float
>     write_policy: Literal["append", "replace", "propose_for_review"]
> 
> @dataclass
> class DeliveryPolicy:
>     mode: Literal["never", "on_material_result", "on_completion",
>                   "on_user_request", "digest"]
>     materiality_threshold: float = 0.0
>     quiet_hours: Any | None = None
>     require_user_opt_in: bool = False
>     max_updates_per_conversation: int = 1
> 
> Caller:
> - plans.py or responders.py creates a Workflow from a selected PlanSpec.
> - scheduler.py identifies runnable operations.
> - workers.py executes operations and emits artifacts/events.
> - reducer.py applies state patches.
> - delivery.py turns eligible artifacts into user-visible updates.
> """
> 
> =====
> planner/workflow/events.py
> """
> Append-only workflow event definitions and event-log interface.
> 
> Events are the audit trail for planning, execution, retries, artifacts, state
> updates, delivery decisions, and learning. State is derived from events rather
> than silently mutated wherever practical.
> 
> Expected event kinds:
> - workflow.created
> - operation.ready / started / completed / failed / cancelled
> - artifact.created
> - state.patch.proposed / applied / rejected
> - continuation.scheduled
> - async.update.delivered
> - feedback.recorded
> - evaluation.completed
> 
> Public API:
> 
> @dataclass(frozen=True)
> class Event:
>     id: str
>     kind: str
>     timestamp: float
>     conversation_id: str
>     workflow_id: str | None
>     operation_id: str | None
>     payload: Mapping[str, Any]
>     provenance: Provenance | None = None
> 
> class EventLog(Protocol):
>     def append(self, event: Event) -> None: ...
>     def read(self, conversation_id: str, after: str | None = None) -> list[Event]: ...
> 
> Caller:
> - planner.py emits workflow/response lifecycle events.
> - workers.py emits execution and artifact events.
> - reducer.py and scheduler.py consume events.
> - persistence.py implements EventLog or delegates it to infrastructure.
> """
> 
> =====
> planner/workflow/reducer.py
> """
> Event-to-state reducer.
> 
> The reducer creates durable, versioned conversation/project/user state from
> append-only events and approved StatePatches. It never rewrites canonical
> chat history.
> 
> Public API:
> 
> class StateReducer:
>     def reduce(self, event: Event) -> None: ...
>     def apply_patch(self, patch: StatePatch) -> None: ...
>     def state_for(self, scope: str, target_id: str) -> Mapping[str, Any]: ...
> 
> Required safeguards:
> - enforce patch scope and write policy;
> - retain provenance/confidence;
> - detect conflicting replacement patches;
> - use review/proposal state for sensitive user-memory writes;
> - make artifacts available to context.py only when eligible.
> 
> Caller:
> - event consumers call `reduce(event)` after event-log persistence.
> - context.py reads reduced state and ready artifacts.
> """
> 
> =====
> planner/workflow/scheduler.py
> """
> Workflow DAG readiness and continuation scheduling.
> 
> The scheduler determines which operations can begin based on completed
> dependencies. Independent operations should run concurrently where supported;
> it must not impose a serial model-tool loop when the selected plan already
> knows necessary work.
> 
> Public API:
> 
> class WorkflowScheduler:
>     def create_workflow(
>         self,
>         plan: PlanSpec,
>         context: PlannerContext,
>         strategy: Literal["wait", "provisional", "deferred"],
>     ) -> Workflow: ...
> 
>     def runnable_operations(self, workflow: Workflow) -> list[Operation]:
>         '''
>         Return pending operations whose required artifact/operation
>         dependencies are completed successfully.
>         '''
>         ...
> 
>     def mark_completed(
>         self,
>         workflow_id: str,
>         operation_id: str,
>         artifacts: Sequence[Artifact],
>         patches: Sequence[StatePatch],
>     ) -> list[Operation]: ...
> 
>     def schedule_continuations(self, event: Event) -> list[Operation]: ...
> 
> Caller:
> - responders.py uses it for synchronous execution of small workflows.
> - background workers use it for deferred/provisional workflows.
> - workers.py receives runnable operations from it.
> - it emits scheduling events through events.py.
> """
> 
> =====
> planner/workflow/workers.py
> """
> Operation execution workers.
> 
> Workers dispatch an Operation to a registered tool, model adapter, evaluator,
> or job handler. They produce typed artifacts and events. A worker must not
> choose plans or mutate transcript history.
> 
> Public API:
> 
> class OperationWorker(Protocol):
>     def execute(
>         self,
>         operation: Operation,
>         *,
>         context: PlannerContext,
>         registry: Registry,
>     ) -> ToolResult | Sequence[Artifact]: ...
> 
> class WorkerDispatcher:
>     def execute_ready_operations(
>         self,
>         workflow: Workflow,
>         operations: Sequence[Operation],
>     ) -> list[Event]: ...
> 
> Caller:
> - scheduler.py identifies readiness.
> - synchronous responders may invoke workers inline.
> - a future queue/worker service invokes the same API asynchronously.
> 
> Failures should be recorded structurally, with retry policy and output
> visibility governed by plan/workflow policy.
> """
> 
> =====
> planner/plans.py
> """
> Plan specification, templating, and instantiation.
> 
> This module defines the structure of a Plan, how PlanSpecs are templated from
> registered models and tools, and how they are instantiated into concrete
> candidate plans ready for selection and execution.
> 
> Important design rules:
> - Plans are immutable after instantiation.
> - Plan identity incorporates model/tool versions, prompts/instructions,
>   responder type, risk policy, and configuration variants that materially
>   affect behavior.
> - Tool configuration is a first-class decision variable.
> - Avoid arbitrary combinatorial model-tool generation. Start with a small,
>   explicit, bounded family of plans.
> - Examples: direct response; model + calculator; model + memory retrieval;
>   model + web/source grounding; draft + independent reviewer.
> 
> Expected helpers:
> 
> class PlanTemplate(Protocol):
>     def instantiate_plan(
>         self,
>         template_id: str,
>         model_specs: Mapping[str, ModelSpec],
>         tool_specs: Mapping[str, ToolSpec],
>         context: PlannerContext,
>         **template_args: Any,
>     ) -> PlanSpec: ...
> 
> def create_direct_plan_template(...) -> PlanTemplate: ...
> def create_tool_use_plan_template(...) -> PlanTemplate: ...
