> 
> ## 5. Correct budget propagation
> 
> Extend `ExecutionBudget`:
> 
> ```python
> @dataclass
> class ExecutionBudget:
>     deadline_monotonic: float
>     remaining_operations: int
>     remaining_tool_cost_usd: float | None = None
>     remaining_depth: int = 3
> ```
> 
> Child budgets should consume from the parent, not copy it:
> 
> ```python
> def reserve_child(self) -> "ExecutionBudget":
>     with self._lock:
>         if self.remaining_depth <= 0:
>             raise JavaScriptExecutionError("Maximum recursive planning depth exceeded.")
>         self.remaining_depth -= 1
>         self.remaining_operations -= 1
>         if self.remaining_operations < 0:
>             raise JavaScriptExecutionError("Execution operation budget exhausted.")
>         return ExecutionBudget(
>             deadline_monotonic=self.deadline_monotonic,
>             remaining_operations=self.remaining_operations,
>             remaining_tool_cost_usd=self.remaining_tool_cost_usd,
>             remaining_depth=self.remaining_depth,
>         )
> ```
> 
> For strict global accounting, use one shared mutable budget object rather than cloned counters.
> 
> ### Tests
> 
> - depth limit rejects grandchildren;
> - multiple concurrent child calls cannot exceed global operation limit;
> - child wall time shares the parent deadline;
> - failed child records `status="failed"` without corrupting its parent run.
> 
